CPU only (no CUDA GPU) | float64 arrays | best of 5 round trips (send -> child has data -> ack)
           N |     MB | A: Queue/pickle (ms) | B1: shm create+copy (ms) | B2: shm zero-copy (ms) |  A / B1 |   A / B2
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     100,000 |    0.8 |                2.066 |                    2.780 |                  1.223 |    0.7x |     1.7x
   1,000,000 |    8.0 |               25.456 |                   10.992 |                  1.340 |    2.3x |    19.0x
  10,000,000 |   80.0 |              238.341 |                   88.338 |                  1.603 |    2.7x |   148.7x

Path A serialises (pickle) and copies the array through a pipe, so cost grows with size.
Path B1 pays one memcpy into shared memory; B2 (producer already writes into shared memory) is truly zero-copy,
so its cost stays roughly constant regardless of N.